and by it less software magazine online at computer.org/software.

5
00:00:15,760 --> 00:00:19,189
[Music]

6
00:00:19,199 --> 00:00:23,750
Hi everyone, this is Priyanka Ragwin for software engineering radio.

7
00:00:23,750 --> 00:00:29,189
and today we will discuss the topic views of AI for debugging.

8
00:00:29,189 --> 00:00:32,069
and we look at three aspects of this show.

9
00:00:32,069 --> 00:00:35,510
One is going to be about using AI as an assistant to debug.

10
00:00:35,510 --> 00:00:41,590
Two AI debugging tools and three is it possible.

11
00:00:41,590 --> 00:00:44,950
that an AI given a bug can help fix and do this autonomously.

12
00:00:44,950 --> 00:00:51,750
For this we have Mark Williamson as a guest and Mark is the CTO for UNDO.

13
00:00:51,750 --> 00:00:59,430
He's also a specialist in kernel level low-level Linux embedded development with a wide experience in crossd disciplinary.

14
00:00:59,440 --> 00:01:01,990
engineering.

15
00:01:01,990 --> 00:01:04,070
He programs a lot in C and C++.

16
00:01:04,070 --> 00:01:08,950
And one of his proudest achievements from the Andu website is his quest towards an all green test.

17
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:12,231
suite. So Mark, welcome to the show.

18
00:01:12,231 --> 00:01:14,310
Is there anything else in your bio.

19
00:01:14,310 --> 00:01:18,149
that you would like to add apart from what I've just introduced you as?

20
00:01:18,159 --> 00:01:20,310
I think that's a pretty good summary.

21
00:01:20,310 --> 00:01:22,710
I guess in my time at Undo,

22
00:01:22,710 --> 00:01:27,270
most of my last 11 years has been a quest to get people to appreciate debuggers more.

23
00:01:27,270 --> 00:01:29,030
and I'm glad to be here talking about them.

24
00:01:29,040 --> 00:01:30,950
They're one of my favorite subjects.

25
00:01:30,960 --> 00:01:33,749
Great.

26
00:01:33,749 --> 00:01:40,870
So, we'll kick off the show by asking you to define debugging in a professional software engineering context.

27
00:01:40,870 --> 00:01:42,789
and how does it differ from simply fixing bugs?

28
00:01:42,799 --> 00:01:44,789
Thanks.

29
00:01:44,789 --> 00:01:48,870
I really like this question because I think it's often misunderstood.

30
00:01:48,880 --> 00:01:54,389
Developers actually spend most of that time at the computer debugging.

31
00:01:54,389 --> 00:01:59,670
It's easy to have a view that bug reports come in from the field from customers.

32
00:01:59,680 --> 00:02:04,069
They go into a GitHub issue tracker or something like that.

33
00:02:04,069 --> 00:02:07,109
They get taken out and a developer fixes the bug.

34
00:02:07,109 --> 00:02:15,350
But I would say debugging is the quest to understand what your program is actually doing.

35
00:02:15,350 --> 00:02:21,270
and why it's not what you expected and that starts the instant you've typed in your first code.

36
00:02:21,270 --> 00:02:26,390
So actually I would say that most of development is itself debugging.

37
00:02:26,390 --> 00:02:32,710
I've seen a lot of stats recently that only about 30% of developer work is programming.

38
00:02:32,710 --> 00:02:37,350
and therefore coding agents aren't solving the whole end to end problem.

39
00:02:37,350 --> 00:02:46,070
But actually I would say that probably 80% of that 30% is debugging not typing in the code.

40
00:02:46,070 --> 00:02:48,949
Code generation is actually a very small part of what developers do.

41
00:02:48,959 --> 00:02:55,750
And a lot of the technical work is this debugging process of answering questions and gaining understanding.

42
00:02:55,760 --> 00:03:00,869
One of the things I wanted to ask you is what do you use debugging for?

43
00:03:00,869 --> 00:03:06,630
So if a program does not function as the way it is supposed to.

44
00:03:06,630 --> 00:03:08,550
then that's called a runtime issue.

45
00:03:08,550 --> 00:03:11,350
Then that would be something that you would debug.

46
00:03:11,350 --> 00:03:16,229
But how about a case when it's not performing very well?

47
00:03:16,229 --> 00:03:19,270
Is that also a case where you would use debugging for?

48
00:03:19,270 --> 00:03:21,910
I would say yes.

49
00:03:21,910 --> 00:03:25,030
I think different developers might call this a different process,

50
00:03:25,030 --> 00:03:30,789
but I would say debugging is anytime you're trying to answer.

51
00:03:30,789 --> 00:03:36,070
what happened or why did that happen and that includes performance issues.

52
00:03:36,070 --> 00:03:40,390
But you have to then broaden your understanding of what a debugging tool is.

53
00:03:40,390 --> 00:03:41,910
So I would say there's lots of tools you can use.

54
00:03:41,910 --> 00:03:43,990
You can add print fs into your code.

55
00:03:43,990 --> 00:03:46,869
They're often logging frameworks.

56
00:03:46,869 --> 00:03:53,350
There's also system level utilities like S trace, GDB, valrind, and Perf.

57
00:03:53,350 --> 00:03:55,910
and actually even also version control in order to go back.

58
00:03:55,920 --> 00:04:00,630
and figure out when a regression came in.

59
00:04:00,630 --> 00:04:02,390
I would say performance is in that continuum.

60
00:04:02,390 --> 00:04:05,030
So you might use a performance profiler,

61
00:04:05,030 --> 00:04:08,869
but actually why did the control flow bring you to the hot path?

62
00:04:08,869 --> 00:04:11,750
Well, that's maybe logging or a debugger.

63
00:04:11,750 --> 00:04:16,630
And it's also a question of using each tool's output to figure out what the next tool you. apply should be.

64
00:04:16,639 --> 00:04:18,550
That's interesting.

65
00:04:18,550 --> 00:04:23,749
This brings me back to one of the episodes we did in SE radio.

66
00:04:23,749 --> 00:04:26,710
which is 3544 which was on debugging.

67
00:04:26,710 --> 00:04:33,189
and the host there had questioned the guest how debugging differs based on the languages paradigms.

68
00:04:33,199 --> 00:04:38,790
whether you're debugging a monolith versus a micros service.

69
00:04:38,790 --> 00:04:40,950
or just the way how you use the tools like as you said.

70
00:04:40,960 --> 00:04:45,270
So in your experience maybe could you talk a little bit about that as well?

71
00:04:45,280 --> 00:04:50,950
Yeah, there is a lot of variation I'd say.

72
00:04:50,950 --> 00:04:56,390
So what I've noticed in the field in my experience is there is a common denominator in everybody's.

73
00:04:56,390 --> 00:04:58,950
debugging experience.

74
00:04:58,950 --> 00:05:01,350
and that is putting more print statements in.

75
00:05:01,350 --> 00:05:05,590
It's probably the first thing you do when you're learning to program and it carries on.

76
00:05:05,590 --> 00:05:08,310
And then there's the grown-up version of putting more print statements in.

77
00:05:08,310 --> 00:05:14,150
which is structured logging and open telemetry and things like that.

78
00:05:14,150 --> 00:05:18,790
I'd say that is common to all languages and all paradigms of programming.

79
00:05:18,790 --> 00:05:26,870
When you get into different more advanced tooling, I think there's often analoges, but it's different.

80
00:05:26,870 --> 00:05:29,749
So most languages have a decent debugger.

81
00:05:29,749 --> 00:05:33,430
and the tool we call a debugger is only one aspect of debugging.

82
00:05:33,430 --> 00:05:37,189
but it typically has some core operations. It lets you step through code.

83
00:05:37,189 --> 00:05:39,590
It lets you print variables.

84
00:05:39,590 --> 00:05:43,670
The way that works is very different depending on your language and your runtime.

85
00:05:43,670 --> 00:05:47,909
So interpreted languages tend to need to tackle these problems very differently to compiled languages.

86
00:05:47,919 --> 00:05:52,390
and different languages have different takes on these as well.

87
00:05:52,390 --> 00:05:59,110
Same goes I think for any kind of mechanical tracing any performance monitoring.

88
00:05:59,110 --> 00:06:04,550
Most of them also have tools that let you time travel debug these days.

89
00:06:04,550 --> 00:06:10,469
But again, the exact implementation and the technique can vary depending on what language you're talking about.

90
00:06:10,469 --> 00:06:15,590
One last point actually is the distributed case where you've got multiple processes.

91
00:06:15,590 --> 00:06:17,990
I would say that is just hard.

92
00:06:17,990 --> 00:06:19,590
One thing about a monolith, it's a lot of code to understand,

93
00:06:19,590 --> 00:06:21,670
but at least it's all in one place.

94
00:06:21,670 --> 00:06:25,029
Once you start having multiple interacting systems,

95
00:06:25,029 --> 00:06:28,950
that is another level of complexity to kind of wrangle and manage.

96
00:06:28,950 --> 00:06:31,110
even though the individual pieces might be simpler.

97
00:06:31,110 --> 00:06:37,110
So I think now let's move on to the part about using AI for debugging.

98
00:06:37,110 --> 00:06:43,670
and it's been now about 2 years where we've been using a lot of LLMs to generate code.

99
00:06:43,670 --> 00:06:50,150
and what do you do once the code is produced you need to debug.

100
00:06:50,150 --> 00:06:54,071
So in your opinion how can AI be used to debug? Is it promising?

101
00:06:54,071 --> 00:06:58,469
What's the I think message from the field and the work that you've been doing.

102
00:06:58,479 --> 00:07:05,670
My sense is that using LLMs for debugging is quite early.

103
00:07:05,670 --> 00:07:09,029
The thing is you mention 2 years.

104
00:07:09,029 --> 00:07:13,110
In some ways it feels like they've been with us forever at this point.

105
00:07:13,110 --> 00:07:18,070
and in other ways every week there's a new announcement or a new change.

106
00:07:18,070 --> 00:07:19,990
So it is literally quite early in the field of debugging.

107
00:07:19,990 --> 00:07:23,110
that goes back to I guess the first computers.

108
00:07:23,110 --> 00:07:29,909
That said, there are lots of places where it looks like LLMs should be good.

109
00:07:29,909 --> 00:07:36,629
So by LLM, I mean large language model based AI, the kind of predominant implementation at the moment.

110
00:07:36,629 --> 00:07:44,870
I would say everything that they can help with in debugging boils down to one of two things.

111
00:07:44,870 --> 00:07:50,390
I reserved my right to change my mind on this, but right now I'd say one, sifting.

112
00:07:50,390 --> 00:07:57,189
So you've got large quantities of maybe partially structured data riffling through it and finding the right bits.

113
00:07:57,189 --> 00:08:00,629
the nuggets that you actually need to know about or identifying patterns.

114
00:08:00,629 --> 00:08:04,070
And the other thing is automation.

115
00:08:04,070 --> 00:08:08,790
So the ability to accomplish a set of tasks that would otherwise require toil from you.

116
00:08:08,790 --> 00:08:13,430
and distract you from the productive work of actually understanding what's going on.

117
00:08:13,440 --> 00:08:19,350
Maybe there's a world where the LLM fully solves the bug for you.

118
00:08:19,350 --> 00:08:25,670
But actually, I think an important thing to remember with all of this is that it's great.

119
00:08:25,670 --> 00:08:27,430
if they can sometimes do a tedious task end to end for you,

120
00:08:27,430 --> 00:08:30,150
but they're tools and their assistants.

121
00:08:30,150 --> 00:08:33,350
So, what we really need to say is how can they help?

122
00:08:33,360 --> 00:08:38,790
So, let's explore the part of the AI being a debugging assistant.

123
00:08:38,790 --> 00:08:44,230
And here I wanted to ask you in your opinion is it more useful for beginners in a.

124
00:08:44,240 --> 00:08:46,310
programming language.

125
00:08:46,310 --> 00:08:48,710
who need guidance to use this assistance.

126
00:08:48,710 --> 00:08:56,230
or is it even good for experienced developers or senior engineers to accelerate complex investigations?

127
00:08:56,240 --> 00:09:03,910
I would almost always answer both for can a tool help beginners and experts.

128
00:09:03,920 --> 00:09:07,430
They potentially help in different ways.

129
00:09:07,440 --> 00:09:12,630
What I'd say is that beginners maybe at programming.

130
00:09:12,630 --> 00:09:17,910
or you can be a beginner anytime you start a new job or move to a new team.

131
00:09:17,910 --> 00:09:22,790
Beginners can benefit from tools that help them manage the complexity of everything they're seeing.

132
00:09:22,800 --> 00:09:25,990
and understand nuances of the code base.

133
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:32,230
or little details that they hadn't appreciated or haven't had time to absorb yet.

134
00:09:32,230 --> 00:09:35,350
So I think there's a lot of potential there for legitimate help.

135
00:09:35,360 --> 00:09:40,870
There is also a shallower help which is also important to everyone at some point.

136
00:09:40,880 --> 00:09:45,829
which is just answer how to do this or please run this tool for me.

137
00:09:45,829 --> 00:09:49,670
I can't be bothered to figure out how to write the bash script that's also valid.

138
00:09:49,670 --> 00:09:51,990
I think for people at any level of expertise.

139
00:09:51,990 --> 00:09:59,910
if you're an expert I think say an expert in programming generally or your chosen domain.

140
00:09:59,910 --> 00:10:02,070
or maybe an expert in a codebase I think there's still help for you.

141
00:10:02,070 --> 00:10:05,190
some of it will be the same you're a beginner every time you go to a new part.

142
00:10:05,190 --> 00:10:07,671
of the codebase. Some of it will be different.

143
00:10:07,680 --> 00:10:14,870
So potentially you'd be using it for more sophisticated questions or more sophisticated automations.

144
00:10:14,870 --> 00:10:20,949
The other dimension in this is are you an expert at prompting.

145
00:10:20,949 --> 00:10:26,630
because all of these LLMs thrive on correct context and high quality context.

146
00:10:26,630 --> 00:10:31,509
And a big part of that is ask them the right question with the right details included.

147
00:10:31,509 --> 00:10:33,590
so they can give you a good answer.

148
00:10:33,590 --> 00:10:36,310
So there's this extra dimension of if you can be good at.

149
00:10:36,310 --> 00:10:39,110
that then you can be better at everything else.

150
00:10:39,120 --> 00:10:41,590
Great.

151
00:10:41,590 --> 00:10:44,069
I really like the line where you said you could be a beginner.

152
00:10:44,069 --> 00:10:46,870
when you're looking at a new piece of code.

153
00:10:46,870 --> 00:10:48,710
through even for an experienced person.

154
00:10:48,710 --> 00:10:55,350
Yeah, I'll go on to this next question which was based on what the answer you just gave.

155
00:10:55,350 --> 00:10:59,829
If you look at wbe coding right now where it's generating large chunks of code.

156
00:10:59,829 --> 00:11:04,870
I've been using it a lot recently for producing some user interface code.

157
00:11:04,870 --> 00:11:07,829
which is not my area of expertise.

158
00:11:07,829 --> 00:11:11,509
And one of the areas which I found very useful was since this generates a lot of code.

159
00:11:11,509 --> 00:11:13,670
and then I run into some problems.

160
00:11:13,670 --> 00:11:20,150
Sometimes I copy paste the messages of the errors onto my VS code screen.

161
00:11:20,150 --> 00:11:24,711
and then I ask the LLM to tell me what might be the cause of this error. And I've seen it's pretty good.

162
00:11:24,720 --> 00:11:31,030
Right now I don't really need to go to Google or Stack Overflow to find this information.

163
00:11:31,030 --> 00:11:33,030
I'm using my coding assistant to help me with that.

164
00:11:33,030 --> 00:11:35,190
In a similar way, I guess for debugging,

165
00:11:35,200 --> 00:11:40,790
would it also make sense that you can copy paste an error.

166
00:11:40,790 --> 00:11:43,750
or some other things from their call log.

167
00:11:43,750 --> 00:11:46,069
and it can help you find out trace out what the problem is?

168
00:11:46,079 --> 00:11:48,870
Yes, I think so.

169
00:11:48,870 --> 00:11:57,911
Where I first found that LLMs were particularly useful in my development flow is effectively a better search. for certain kind of things.

170
00:11:57,911 --> 00:12:03,269
A much superior search to what I could do for Google.

171
00:12:03,269 --> 00:12:06,389
and the kind of problems that I would find it applied best to is.

172
00:12:06,389 --> 00:12:09,430
where I want to search.

173
00:12:09,430 --> 00:12:14,710
not just on keywords but on the meaning of the keywords and the context of the keywords.

174
00:12:14,710 --> 00:12:20,629
So my previous approach to that had to be hope that somebody has put all the keywords together.

175
00:12:20,629 --> 00:12:25,990
with the context that's relevant in Stack Overflow and then my Google search finds it often.

176
00:12:25,990 --> 00:12:31,509
Now I can ask an LLM and I can include a lot of semantic information.

177
00:12:31,509 --> 00:12:33,430
So I can say this is what I'm trying to achieve.

178
00:12:33,430 --> 00:12:35,750
or this is what I believe the code is doing.

179
00:12:35,750 --> 00:12:37,990
This is the message that I'm dealing with.

180
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:42,389
Please give me the relevant information for that case.

181
00:12:42,389 --> 00:12:45,030
And because at least in my very crude understanding of LLMs.

182
00:12:45,040 --> 00:12:51,910
they are translating all the tokens I gave into some sort of highdimensional meaning space.

183
00:12:51,910 --> 00:12:56,949
they can find the thing which means what I meant very very effectively.

184
00:12:56,949 --> 00:13:01,750
So yes, I think they are potentially fantastic for that sort of thing.

185
00:13:01,750 --> 00:13:03,750
Once you bring in coding agents and the ability to act on your system.

186
00:13:03,760 --> 00:13:05,670
and act on your codebase as well.

187
00:13:05,670 --> 00:13:09,590
they have the ability to search your codebase for relevant information.

188
00:13:09,590 --> 00:13:11,910
and populate that context window with other stuff.

189
00:13:11,920 --> 00:13:15,910
And then that's maybe another dimension to debugging more effectively.

190
00:13:15,920 --> 00:13:17,829
Okay.

191
00:13:17,829 --> 00:13:21,030
So the searching is one angle where your debugging assistant can help.

192
00:13:21,040 --> 00:13:27,430
The other angle which I wanted to ask you was LLMs.

193
00:13:27,430 --> 00:13:31,590
and coding agents are now being used to generate a lot of test cases.

194
00:13:31,590 --> 00:13:34,550
Could this also be used for debugging assistance?

195
00:13:34,550 --> 00:13:39,269
And here I have an example where suppose I have a null pointer exception in a service running.

196
00:13:39,279 --> 00:13:42,150
in production.

197
00:13:42,150 --> 00:13:45,750
Would an LLM assisted test case help me narrow down the cause?

198
00:13:45,760 --> 00:13:48,949
I think so.

199
00:13:48,949 --> 00:13:55,430
The challenge with test cases is often getting them written at all.

200
00:13:55,430 --> 00:13:59,189
A lot of developers nowadays, I think, appreciate test-driven development.

201
00:13:59,189 --> 00:14:05,590
And so I think the situation for tests is a lot better than it was.

202
00:14:05,590 --> 00:14:07,829
but still it is a truism that things are underested.

203
00:14:07,829 --> 00:14:09,509
Tests are not written when they should be.

204
00:14:09,509 --> 00:14:11,670
The discipline is important.

205
00:14:11,670 --> 00:14:17,350
So I think the first thing that LLMs might do is help us populate our tests sooner.

206
00:14:17,350 --> 00:14:20,230
so that these problems don't get out there.

207
00:14:20,230 --> 00:14:25,430
But certainly once you've got something in production, you've got an issue you need to replicate.

208
00:14:25,430 --> 00:14:30,629
feels intuitively reasonable that LLMs could get involved all the way along.

209
00:14:30,629 --> 00:14:32,710
So I would think this is actually more of a continuum.

210
00:14:32,720 --> 00:14:37,590
probably you might use the LLM to help write a test case in the first place.

211
00:14:37,590 --> 00:14:39,509
and try and try and provoke a bug.

212
00:14:39,509 --> 00:14:46,389
or actually also usefully write tests for things you suspect are the problem and rule them out.

213
00:14:46,389 --> 00:14:51,990
But even then, once you've maybe managed to replicate something, you've still got to understand it.

214
00:14:51,990 --> 00:14:55,189
And at that point, what you need is your full suite of tooling.

215
00:14:55,199 --> 00:15:00,069
So there's log analysis again, there's performance analysis, there's debuggers.

216
00:15:00,079 --> 00:15:09,590
Another thing then that LLMs could do for you there is help bring together all of those tools.

217
00:15:09,590 --> 00:15:14,950
And by the way, I'd say testing is another part of the large definition of debugging I gave. earlier.

218
00:15:14,959 --> 00:15:19,509
and writing tests because it helps you understand what and why of the code.

219
00:15:19,509 --> 00:15:21,509
I think there's two sides.

220
00:15:21,509 --> 00:15:24,470
So AI can make things a lot worse for us in the sense.

221
00:15:24,480 --> 00:15:28,790
that you don't need 30 years of development.

222
00:15:28,790 --> 00:15:30,470
and a team of a thousand people to make a legacy codebase.

223
00:15:30,470 --> 00:15:32,710
You can vibe it now.

224
00:15:32,710 --> 00:15:35,750
but I can also make it a lot better by taking away toil.

225
00:15:35,750 --> 00:15:41,350
but also by giving us a smoother transition between tools.

226
00:15:41,350 --> 00:15:43,829
So LLMs are actually very eager to use tools these days.

227
00:15:43,829 --> 00:15:49,590
and they don't have the psychological barriers to learning them that humans do.

228
00:15:49,590 --> 00:15:53,749
So maybe in this case you could say well LLM I have a problem here.

229
00:15:53,749 --> 00:15:56,231
Please write a test case to replicate it. It's done that.

230
00:15:56,231 --> 00:16:00,629
It deploys maybe some more logging into production for you.

231
00:16:00,629 --> 00:16:05,670
and you isolate more closely by analyzing the logs that come out.

232
00:16:05,670 --> 00:16:10,150
Then perhaps you have to investigate that in more detail outside production.

233
00:16:10,150 --> 00:16:14,550
Maybe using a debugger more detailed logging again you allow the LLM to iterate on it.

234
00:16:14,550 --> 00:16:16,949
So it can potentially help you all the way through this flow.

235
00:16:16,959 --> 00:16:23,430
and take away a load of things that individually would have been distractions to the task of understanding.

236
00:16:23,440 --> 00:16:25,031
I like that. That's great.

237
00:16:25,031 --> 00:16:30,310
So you can have almost have an LLM as your interface between the different tools.

238
00:16:30,320 --> 00:16:34,470
and help you find stuff and then pipe it back to another tool.

239
00:16:34,470 --> 00:16:40,710
and help with the understanding of the debugging problem that you're trying to solve.

240
00:16:40,710 --> 00:16:44,150
So, let's talk about debugging strategies.

241
00:16:44,150 --> 00:16:48,870
Is that something that your AI debugging assistant can help with?

242
00:16:48,880 --> 00:16:52,389
I think yes.

243
00:16:52,389 --> 00:16:58,310
And actually, one very simple way we found they can help is they're a better rubber duck.

244
00:16:58,310 --> 00:17:02,629
In our office, developers are actually we've got some rubber ducks.

245
00:17:02,629 --> 00:17:04,150
that used to lie around in the office.

246
00:17:04,150 --> 00:17:07,909
developers have actually used those and tried to explain an issue to them.

247
00:17:07,909 --> 00:17:10,949
and in the process solved it.

248
00:17:10,949 --> 00:17:14,549
Imagine if the rubber duck had a lot of software engineering expertise as well.

249
00:17:14,549 --> 00:17:16,710
You could just bounce ideas back and forth.

250
00:17:16,710 --> 00:17:20,549
So I think that's the first step actually is just giving you ideas.

251
00:17:20,549 --> 00:17:22,470
that you wouldn't have thought of on your own.

252
00:17:22,470 --> 00:17:25,669
Doesn't have to solve the thing for you.

253
00:17:25,669 --> 00:17:30,230
Then identifying different possible tools and different ways of applying things is another one.

254
00:17:30,240 --> 00:17:39,510
in the maybe slightly longer term as people are using LLM based agents more as well.

255
00:17:39,510 --> 00:17:42,310
We touched on how the AI could be your core interface to things.

256
00:17:42,310 --> 00:17:50,950
and I think one of the challenges in a debugging strategy is staying in your flow state.

257
00:17:50,950 --> 00:17:56,710
So there's a reason that people love logging and it's because it's programming and they're already doing that.

258
00:17:56,710 --> 00:17:58,549
So you've just written some code, you want to know what it did,

259
00:17:58,549 --> 00:18:00,230
you can type in a few more lines.

260
00:18:00,230 --> 00:18:04,150
That might take if you've got a big C++ codebase, it might take a few hours to rebuild.

261
00:18:04,160 --> 00:18:08,070
You go and have a sword fight on a desk chair, but you're still programming.

262
00:18:08,080 --> 00:18:09,990
You're still in your flow state.

263
00:18:09,990 --> 00:18:18,071
I think a potentially very valuable thing AI agents could do is once your flow state is a. conversation with the agent.

264
00:18:18,080 --> 00:18:23,029
transitioning much more seamlessly into other debugging strategies.

265
00:18:23,029 --> 00:18:27,510
So instead of you having to get your head out of coding space.

266
00:18:27,510 --> 00:18:29,990
and think about Perf or think about GDB.

267
00:18:29,990 --> 00:18:35,510
or think about whatever your logging framework is even if it's complicated.

268
00:18:35,510 --> 00:18:37,510
just say okay, what should I do next?

269
00:18:37,510 --> 00:18:39,510
Please look at the performance logs.

270
00:18:39,510 --> 00:18:44,311
please gather a time travel recording and correlate them for me. And you stay in your flow.

271
00:18:44,311 --> 00:18:51,270
You stay in your vibing mindset rather than having to transition between all of these different command syntaxes.

272
00:18:51,270 --> 00:18:53,350
and output formats, etc.

273
00:18:53,360 --> 00:18:55,510
That's interesting.

274
00:18:55,510 --> 00:19:00,230
So, a way to maintain your context or you don't have to do so much context switching, right?

275
00:19:00,240 --> 00:19:01,751
Exactly. Yeah.

276
00:19:01,760 --> 00:19:03,830
That's great.

277
00:19:03,830 --> 00:19:06,549
Since I've got you on the show, I had to ask this question.

278
00:19:06,549 --> 00:19:12,150
Kernel level bugs are supposed to be very difficult to fix.

279
00:19:12,150 --> 00:19:13,830
Can a debug assistant help with this?

280
00:19:13,840 --> 00:19:17,510
I think yes.

281
00:19:17,510 --> 00:19:20,310
In my programming and kernel level stuff,

282
00:19:20,310 --> 00:19:27,029
it's mostly been on the Linux kernel or on Linux derived kernel level code.

283
00:19:27,029 --> 00:19:30,549
And I've not yet tried applying an LLM to that.

284
00:19:30,549 --> 00:19:33,590
But my expectation would be that it will actually be a very good experience.

285
00:19:33,600 --> 00:19:40,870
because the LLM is potentially in its training data set encountered parts of Linux code.

286
00:19:40,870 --> 00:19:46,230
It certainly will have encountered documentation about it, mailing list, discussions, etc.

287
00:19:46,230 --> 00:19:48,390
So it will know the context about kernel code.

288
00:19:48,400 --> 00:19:53,510
that I do not or that isn't in my head right now.

289
00:19:53,510 --> 00:19:55,350
And then it's also very good at understanding a big complex code base.

290
00:19:55,360 --> 00:19:57,990
which of course kernels typically are.

291
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:03,590
So I can see it being very helpful from that side,

292
00:20:03,590 --> 00:20:07,350
maybe even for generating some of the code if you can get it to understand the right rules.

293
00:20:07,350 --> 00:20:11,190
There's a lot of written and unwritten rules in kernel programming.

294
00:20:11,190 --> 00:20:16,470
but if you can get those in place, I think it could be very useful there.

295
00:20:16,470 --> 00:20:22,950
The thing that I'm not aware of anyone having tried is trying to automate your debugging flow.

296
00:20:22,950 --> 00:20:31,270
So possibly within easy reach would be add some logging statements, rebuild and reboot some remote machine.

297
00:20:31,270 --> 00:20:32,789
and then see what comes out.

298
00:20:32,789 --> 00:20:35,270
I think you could do that.

299
00:20:35,270 --> 00:20:41,029
The really spicy thing I think would be hooking up an LLM to a kernel mode debugger.

300
00:20:41,029 --> 00:20:43,190
and having it step through the kernel code on another machine.

301
00:20:43,190 --> 00:20:47,110
I really haven't heard of anyone doing that.

302
00:20:47,110 --> 00:20:49,029
I'd love to find out if anyone has because that sounds.

303
00:20:49,029 --> 00:20:50,870
well it sounds awesome.

304
00:20:50,870 --> 00:20:54,549
It also sounds like an absolute nightmare to manage. So I'd be very interested to see what could do there.

305
00:20:54,559 --> 00:20:57,990
But eventually I imagine that's what it will be like.

306
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:02,149
So now that we've looked at that, I wanted to ask you another question.

307
00:21:02,149 --> 00:21:08,950
When we always talk about LLM use cases that we've seen on literature and also on blog posts.

308
00:21:08,950 --> 00:21:15,270
even for the debugging aspects the languages are predominantly in like Python, JavaScript, Go, Java.

309
00:21:15,280 --> 00:21:22,549
I've never seen that much about C and C++.

310
00:21:22,549 --> 00:21:26,470
What is your experience with using AI assistance for coding.

311
00:21:26,470 --> 00:21:30,549
as well as say debugging C and C++ code?

312
00:21:30,549 --> 00:21:33,909
These days a lot of my coding is actually in Python.

313
00:21:33,919 --> 00:21:38,710
and in sort of the glue levels above these low-level systems.

314
00:21:38,710 --> 00:21:44,230
So I've been using coding assistants in various forms to help me with.

315
00:21:44,230 --> 00:21:47,270
that and I found it it's very useful.

316
00:21:47,270 --> 00:21:51,270
One of the advantages I believe that LLMs have for languages.

317
00:21:51,280 --> 00:21:55,669
that are perhaps more modern and perhaps more scripting oriented.

318
00:21:55,669 --> 00:22:00,630
is that there's a lot of code out in the public they can be trained on.

319
00:22:00,630 --> 00:22:02,710
So they are excellent at understanding those languages.

320
00:22:02,710 --> 00:22:08,789
The flip side is that I've also heard I haven't experienced this myself.

321
00:22:08,789 --> 00:22:15,510
but they can get a bit muddled with what is valid code in a dynamic language.

322
00:22:15,510 --> 00:22:22,952
So languages like JavaScript and Python, you don't have the guard rails of the compiler telling you, "No, don't do that. That's wrong."

323
00:22:22,960 --> 00:22:30,470
When you do something bad with the type system, and that's potentially a weakness.

324
00:22:30,470 --> 00:22:33,590
So the nice thing, I guess, for compiled languages like C.

325
00:22:33,590 --> 00:22:35,750
and C++ is that you do have the compiler there.

326
00:22:35,750 --> 00:22:40,630
to give the LLM a telling off and say, "No, you can't do that.

327
00:22:40,630 --> 00:22:43,351
That doesn't type check. Try it again."

328
00:22:43,351 --> 00:22:47,590
And it gives the LLM some guard rails, which is always good.

329
00:22:47,590 --> 00:22:53,351
I think one of the things they need is to be grounded in some sort of truth about. the system.

330
00:22:53,351 --> 00:22:56,950
so that they can keep being pulled back to that rather than hallucinating.

331
00:22:56,950 --> 00:23:01,830
And the other thing they need is good quality context about what they're doing.

332
00:23:01,830 --> 00:23:05,990
and what's going on right now.

333
00:23:05,990 --> 00:23:08,630
So in terms of the context actually my experience is.

334
00:23:08,630 --> 00:23:13,909
that coding agents already pretty good at finding that context.

335
00:23:13,909 --> 00:23:17,190
in I suspect any languages code base. They know how to navigate different programming languages.

336
00:23:17,190 --> 00:23:21,029
They know how to navigate a project structure.

337
00:23:21,029 --> 00:23:23,430
And there's enough C and C++ out there.

338
00:23:23,430 --> 00:23:27,990
that they are decently good at generating it and understanding it as well.

339
00:23:27,990 --> 00:23:33,350
I guess it's possible that there are some shortcomings I haven't seen yet.

340
00:23:33,350 --> 00:23:35,590
but certainly they seem effective from everything I've tried.

341
00:23:35,590 --> 00:23:39,270
The only thing I'd say is that C.

342
00:23:39,270 --> 00:23:44,310
and C++ tend to also be associated with big scary legacy code bases.

343
00:23:44,310 --> 00:23:46,390
and they tend to have very unfortunate patterns of bugs.

344
00:23:46,390 --> 00:23:52,310
and they tend not to have standard logging frameworks and so it does create a load of challenges.

345
00:23:52,310 --> 00:23:53,830
you might not see in other languages.

346
00:23:53,840 --> 00:23:58,070
Mhm.

347
00:23:58,070 --> 00:24:00,549
like heap uh errors and memory buffer overflows and all that good stuff.

348
00:24:00,549 --> 00:24:03,910
Yeah, exactly. Yes.

349
00:24:03,910 --> 00:24:10,070
So, so few rules in C and C++ compared to what you can rely on in other programming. languages. It's part of what makes it fun.

350
00:24:10,070 --> 00:24:15,190
and it's part of what makes it effective for kernel level programming. but it is a double-edged sword.

351
00:24:15,200 --> 00:24:17,029
Okay.

352
00:24:17,029 --> 00:24:20,149
So, let's now go into some of the tooling for debugging.

353
00:24:20,149 --> 00:24:21,990
And one of the things that actually you pointed me to.

354
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:26,710
when I was researching for this show was this tool called chat dbg.

355
00:24:26,710 --> 00:24:29,590
I don't know if is it chat debugger or chat dbg.

356
00:24:29,590 --> 00:24:32,630
What is it? Maybe could you explain that to our listeners?

357
00:24:32,640 --> 00:24:37,830
Sure.

358
00:24:37,830 --> 00:24:39,669
So chat dbg is a research paper originally.

359
00:24:39,669 --> 00:24:46,549
The title of the research paper is augmenting debugging with large language models.

360
00:24:46,549 --> 00:24:52,630
and that's out of the University of Massachusetts, Amhurst, AWS, and Williams College.

361
00:24:52,630 --> 00:24:57,669
What they did was hook up various software debuggers.

362
00:24:57,679 --> 00:25:04,710
the conventional forward stepping variable printing debuggers we are all used to.

363
00:25:04,710 --> 00:25:08,390
and have all used at some point to LLM.

364
00:25:08,390 --> 00:25:12,070
And I think back when they actually published this originally,

365
00:25:12,070 --> 00:25:16,950
they were using the new tool calling abilities of LLM.

366
00:25:16,950 --> 00:25:19,269
So one of the things that's become,

367
00:25:19,269 --> 00:25:25,669
I think, quite revolutionary in AI in the last year or two.

368
00:25:25,669 --> 00:25:28,310
is the ability for the AI to call external tools.

369
00:25:28,310 --> 00:25:33,269
And that gives the AI the ability to populate its own context window with relevant things.

370
00:25:33,269 --> 00:25:37,590
and to access the ground truth about the outside world.

371
00:25:37,590 --> 00:25:45,110
So what they've done is they said, well, what if the LLM had access to a software debugger.

372
00:25:45,110 --> 00:25:51,110
and now it can monitor the behavior of the code using.

373
00:25:51,110 --> 00:25:53,510
that software debugger and gain deeper insights into it.

374
00:25:53,510 --> 00:25:55,110
And moreover, what if we then say,

375
00:25:55,110 --> 00:26:01,590
well, the user can ask questions not about how to run the debugger.

376
00:26:01,590 --> 00:26:05,110
but about the actual behavior of the program itself.

377
00:26:05,110 --> 00:26:09,830
So eventually you can just ask one of their examples, why is X null here?

378
00:26:09,830 --> 00:26:15,430
So it's natural language, which is nice, but it's also a higher level kind of question.

379
00:26:15,430 --> 00:26:21,510
You're not having to compose the operations required in the debugger to answer the question.

380
00:26:21,510 --> 00:26:23,669
You just say the thing you want to know.

381
00:26:23,669 --> 00:26:29,029
and it's almost more like a query than operating an interactive tool. Now, well, that's great.

382
00:26:29,029 --> 00:26:33,510
So, it's like when you're debugging in your call stack, you can have like a natural language.

383
00:26:33,510 --> 00:26:36,710
where you can pose a question through the LLM.

384
00:26:36,720 --> 00:26:40,150
and then it'll find it out and reply back to you. Right.

385
00:26:40,159 --> 00:26:43,909
Exactly. Yes. Okay, that's cool.

386
00:26:43,909 --> 00:26:45,909
I think something that we would all be looking forward to.

387
00:26:45,919 --> 00:26:53,990
And taking off from there, one of the questions I had based on the previous answers.

388
00:26:53,990 --> 00:26:57,590
where you said it's possible for the AI to actually go between languages.

389
00:26:57,600 --> 00:27:01,990
and almost also between different tooling, right?

390
00:27:01,990 --> 00:27:07,350
If you had a very large system that's built on different components like what we typically have nowadays.

391
00:27:07,350 --> 00:27:09,510
we have some scripting in Python, we have a back end in Java,

392
00:27:09,510 --> 00:27:14,789
we have a front end in Vue.js or React or whatever.

393
00:27:14,789 --> 00:27:16,950
And usually a bug kind of spans between all these boundaries.

394
00:27:16,950 --> 00:27:26,149
Do you think something like this chat DBG could help us track bugs across multiple languages.

395
00:27:26,149 --> 00:27:29,909
and then show us a recommended approach to fix the problem in an affected module?

396
00:27:29,919 --> 00:27:32,230
I think that would be very interesting.

397
00:27:32,240 --> 00:27:40,630
I'm not aware of currently any AI agent that will combine all of the complicated parts of that.

398
00:27:40,630 --> 00:27:46,789
So the multiple languages, the distributed nature of it, complex interactions.

399
00:27:46,789 --> 00:27:51,110
Chat DBG, I think it has several different debugger backends.

400
00:27:51,110 --> 00:27:53,750
So you could maybe imagine it talking to a C component.

401
00:27:53,750 --> 00:27:55,750
and to a Python component and to some other components.

402
00:27:55,760 --> 00:28:03,990
The challenge for debugging a distributed system though is also that you need to allow it to run.

403
00:28:03,990 --> 00:28:09,190
So using live debuggers that step can be difficult in a distributed system.

404
00:28:09,190 --> 00:28:12,549
even when you've solved the problems of can I cover all the languages.

405
00:28:12,549 --> 00:28:14,549
that I need can I understand the interactions.

406
00:28:14,549 --> 00:28:19,269
because if you stop one of them then timeouts can happen.

407
00:28:19,269 --> 00:28:22,230
or you can change you can radically change the order in which things happen.

408
00:28:22,230 --> 00:28:26,310
So it's a it's a challenging area.

409
00:28:26,310 --> 00:28:30,070
I also suspect that for quite a.

410
00:28:30,070 --> 00:28:34,471
while doing this well this sort of very varied problem is still going to need human guidance as. well.

411
00:28:34,480 --> 00:28:39,750
because there's a lot of different things you need the LLM to be smart for.

412
00:28:39,760 --> 00:28:46,390
and my general experience has been it's best to give it one thing to be smart about at. once.

413
00:28:46,390 --> 00:28:52,711
Trying to get it to balance lots of different tasks from lots of different sources without some guard. rails is challenging.

414
00:28:52,711 --> 00:28:57,190
So you either need guardrails to come from your system.

415
00:28:57,190 --> 00:28:58,950
or you need them to come from the human.

416
00:28:58,950 --> 00:29:01,750
and I think it's it's going to be a case of both of those for some time to.

417
00:29:01,760 --> 00:29:03,669
come. Yeah.

418
00:29:03,669 --> 00:29:05,909
So I think that is a bit of a complicated use case.

419
00:29:05,909 --> 00:29:09,029
but it's maybe something that will be solved in the future.

420
00:29:09,029 --> 00:29:14,071
I'll move on to the next question which is I wanted to know about time travel debugging. What is it?

421
00:29:14,071 --> 00:29:17,430
time travel debugging.

422
00:29:17,430 --> 00:29:20,310
It's a vision for how you should debug software first of all.

423
00:29:20,320 --> 00:29:26,549
And the vision is that you shouldn't have to pick.

424
00:29:26,549 --> 00:29:28,470
and choose what information you get like you do with logging.

425
00:29:28,470 --> 00:29:30,870
You should have it all by default.

426
00:29:30,870 --> 00:29:37,909
So what time travel debuggers have in common is the ability to record everything your program did.

427
00:29:37,909 --> 00:29:39,430
and then replay it deterministically.

428
00:29:39,440 --> 00:29:43,750
Typically they can do that in reverse as well.

429
00:29:43,750 --> 00:29:45,669
So you can actually rewind which I'll come back to.

430
00:29:45,669 --> 00:29:47,750
The trick with time travel debugging is making it efficient.

431
00:29:47,760 --> 00:29:52,630
Modern time travel debugging systems are actually very efficient.

432
00:29:52,630 --> 00:29:57,910
So they don't need to single step the program and record every instruction that ran anymore. That would be very bad.

433
00:29:57,910 --> 00:30:01,510
That would be, you know, higher overhead than detailed logging.

434
00:30:01,510 --> 00:30:06,950
What they do instead is they use a variety of lower level tricks in the system.

435
00:30:06,950 --> 00:30:11,590
to capture only what affects the nondeterministic behaviors at execution time.

436
00:30:11,590 --> 00:30:16,630
and then replay just those and you can recomputee every intermediate state.

437
00:30:16,630 --> 00:30:23,831
So it means every memory location at every machine instruction that ran is available to you now. and what you need to do is.

438
00:30:23,831 --> 00:30:29,669
then select the variables you want and that's where the reverse execution comes in.

439
00:30:29,669 --> 00:30:35,430
So I like to say that normal debuggers tell you what because they're like a microscope.

440
00:30:35,430 --> 00:30:37,669
They let you inspect all of the state in your program.

441
00:30:37,669 --> 00:30:40,070
and understand exactly what is going on right now.

442
00:30:40,070 --> 00:30:46,310
A time travel debugger gives you access to causality I guess.

443
00:30:46,310 --> 00:30:51,350
So you can say how did we get here and that means taking you from what to why.

444
00:30:51,350 --> 00:30:55,909
So the real big benefit is to be able to query backwards in time.

445
00:30:55,909 --> 00:30:59,350
and say well how did this value get set in the past?

446
00:30:59,350 --> 00:31:05,190
How did we get into this function call and why did we get in now?

447
00:31:05,190 --> 00:31:10,071
So, it's a very broad set of data almost in a way the broadest set of data you. could have about your program.

448
00:31:10,071 --> 00:31:15,669
and you query it to answer questions all the way from conventional debugging problems.

449
00:31:15,679 --> 00:31:22,470
to performance problems to stuff that you might otherwise have used logging for but that needs a rebuild.

450
00:31:22,480 --> 00:31:24,230
Okay, great.

451
00:31:24,230 --> 00:31:30,389
So does it work with the trace data that that we usually use like our logs and traces? Does it work with that?

452
00:31:30,399 --> 00:31:38,549
Time travel debug systems usually they work at a lower level than that typically.

453
00:31:38,549 --> 00:31:43,590
So there are some time travel like systems which use something like trace data to reconstruct states.

454
00:31:43,590 --> 00:31:48,950
But the trouble is in those systems you can only reconstruct what was traced.

455
00:31:48,950 --> 00:31:51,909
That's often not everything.

456
00:31:51,909 --> 00:31:56,549
So time travel debugging systems tend to be implemented at a lower level.

457
00:31:56,549 --> 00:32:01,190
either at the level of the programming languages runtime particularly for interpreted languages.

458
00:32:01,200 --> 00:32:06,630
or as some sort of just in time recompilation for native languages.

459
00:32:06,630 --> 00:32:10,549
So they tend to sit under the level of your code.

460
00:32:10,549 --> 00:32:16,389
and that's what gives them the power to inspect and capture everything it does efficiently.

461
00:32:16,389 --> 00:32:18,310
What you can do is combine techniques.

462
00:32:18,310 --> 00:32:22,789
So potentially you could take a time travel debuggers recording.

463
00:32:22,789 --> 00:32:27,269
and you could extract the same information you usually would have got from tracing.

464
00:32:27,269 --> 00:32:33,029
Is there a lot of plumbing that needs to be done to support this or typically no actually.

465
00:32:33,029 --> 00:32:38,230
the integrations with time travel debuggers are very simple.

466
00:32:38,230 --> 00:32:42,149
and I'd say it's for similar reasons to the phenomenon where you say.

467
00:32:42,159 --> 00:32:45,909
well I want to run my code in a virtual machine now.

468
00:32:45,909 --> 00:32:47,990
or I want to run my code in the container now.

469
00:32:47,990 --> 00:32:49,990
and you just lift it up and put it there and it works.

470
00:32:49,990 --> 00:32:55,830
The fact that the integration of a time travel debugging system is below the level of your code.

471
00:32:55,830 --> 00:32:58,470
means you don't explicitly need to change anything.

472
00:32:58,470 --> 00:33:03,269
You just feed an extra layer into the system and you get that extra visibility.

473
00:33:03,279 --> 00:33:05,351
Okay. Interesting.

474
00:33:05,351 --> 00:33:09,350
So it's like another question cuz I'm a bit fascinated with this is the fact.

475
00:33:09,350 --> 00:33:11,750
that it keeps track of say.

476
00:33:11,750 --> 00:33:15,750
is it at the register level like what gets written to the register something like that.

477
00:33:15,750 --> 00:33:21,190
or a bit more higher for time travel debugging systems that work at machine instruction level.

478
00:33:21,190 --> 00:33:26,950
Yes, it's it's register level state and memory level.

479
00:33:26,950 --> 00:33:28,630
But the important thing is tracking that would be horrible.

480
00:33:28,640 --> 00:33:35,029
Tracking your register state for every machine instruction would be a nightmare.

481
00:33:35,029 --> 00:33:40,710
So what they actually do in practice and this is true across a variety of systems.

482
00:33:40,710 --> 00:33:44,950
is they capture what was the starting state of your program at a low level.

483
00:33:44,950 --> 00:33:50,230
So the registers and memory, what information got into your program from the outside world.

484
00:33:50,230 --> 00:33:52,950
and then everything else can be recomputed.

485
00:33:52,950 --> 00:33:57,110
And there's a load of clever tricks you do to make the recomputation efficient.

486
00:33:57,120 --> 00:34:01,830
because you don't want to replay everything you recorded every time you want to ask a question.

487
00:34:01,830 --> 00:34:07,590
But fundamentally, you actually only need to know what influenced the runtime.

488
00:34:07,590 --> 00:34:15,589
because modern CPUs are immensely good at rerunning deterministic code very very quickly.

489
00:34:15,589 --> 00:34:19,430
You don't need to be capturing all of that stuff and actually it's lower overhead not to.

490
00:34:19,430 --> 00:34:21,190
So, it's actually it's smoke and mirrors.

491
00:34:21,190 --> 00:34:27,430
We call it time travel debugging, but the real technique underneath the hood is deterministic record and replay.

492
00:34:27,430 --> 00:34:32,389
And then everything else is kind of magic tricks to provide a better user interface.

493
00:34:32,389 --> 00:34:33,990
so that it looks like a debugger.

494
00:34:33,990 --> 00:34:37,669
or it looks like a logging system or it looks like a tool an AI agent can use.

495
00:34:37,679 --> 00:34:45,431
That's great because I would like to just end this section of by asking you a question. which I saw on the Undo website.

496
00:34:45,440 --> 00:34:51,510
which is that would you be able to point me to what caused a crash 15 years back.

497
00:34:51,520 --> 00:34:55,750
and the developer who wrote the code has left the company?

498
00:34:55,750 --> 00:34:59,430
Could time travel debugging help with this kind of a problem?

499
00:34:59,440 --> 00:35:02,310
Absolutely.

500
00:35:02,310 --> 00:35:08,230
So, I think the reason that that's a good example is because it's legacy code.

501
00:35:08,230 --> 00:35:11,510
It's a huge system and it's something.

502
00:35:11,510 --> 00:35:15,990
that just starts to happen particularly in big organizations when they've been developing for a while.

503
00:35:15,990 --> 00:35:24,870
And it happens even in the most or perhaps especially the most missionritical important code bases people have.

504
00:35:24,870 --> 00:35:28,950
because over time you have thousands of people work on these.

505
00:35:28,950 --> 00:35:33,990
they work in different generations of programming languages different paradigms.

506
00:35:33,990 --> 00:35:36,630
and there's lots of domain specific expertise.

507
00:35:36,630 --> 00:35:41,430
and as we said earlier anytime you go into code that you didn't write you're a beginner again.

508
00:35:41,440 --> 00:35:46,310
particularly if it's a large body of work.

509
00:35:46,310 --> 00:35:52,070
So the reason that time travel debugging helps in these cases is it allows you to see the. causality.

510
00:35:52,070 --> 00:35:57,829
So you don't have to understand your 10 million line code base in detail to infer how a.

511
00:35:57,839 --> 00:36:00,630
bug happened.

512
00:36:00,630 --> 00:36:03,109
Instead, you can rewind through it.

513
00:36:03,109 --> 00:36:06,711
So you could say, well, this value was bad. Why was it bad?

514
00:36:06,711 --> 00:36:08,791
Rewind to where it last changed. Oh, okay.

515
00:36:08,791 --> 00:36:10,470
I didn't expect to be in that code path.

516
00:36:10,480 --> 00:36:12,470
Why are we there?

517
00:36:12,470 --> 00:36:16,870
And so you can rewind again and find why the decisions were taken there.

518
00:36:16,870 --> 00:36:19,990
And what it means is that a lot of the domain specific knowledge.

519
00:36:20,000 --> 00:36:24,550
that you might have needed to ask your colleague.

520
00:36:24,550 --> 00:36:29,589
who left 15 years ago actually can be recaptured by understanding what really happened.

521
00:36:29,589 --> 00:36:31,670
And stuff you didn't need to know.

522
00:36:31,670 --> 00:36:35,109
Like theories you had about the bug that were wrong, you don't need to worry about anymore.

523
00:36:35,109 --> 00:36:37,430
because you can see that those things didn't happen.

524
00:36:37,440 --> 00:36:42,710
The interesting thing here, and it took us a while to realize this even at Undo.

525
00:36:42,720 --> 00:36:50,311
is that the problem we're managing for developers here is very similar to the problems you're managing for. an AI.

526
00:36:50,311 --> 00:36:54,310
So, it's provide them with a ground truth of what really happened in the system.

527
00:36:54,320 --> 00:36:56,550
Provide them tools to navigate it.

528
00:36:56,560 --> 00:37:01,990
Provide them with the right context and highquality relevant context.

529
00:37:01,990 --> 00:37:06,390
And don't give them irrelevant information because it will confuse them.

530
00:37:06,390 --> 00:37:10,310
is actually very very similar phenomena to what we all have.

531
00:37:10,310 --> 00:37:12,870
when we're trying to get good output from AIs.

532
00:37:12,870 --> 00:37:15,510
It's just that humans are much better intelligences.

533
00:37:15,510 --> 00:37:19,430
and so you know the levels of context they can cope with are smaller.

534
00:37:19,430 --> 00:37:23,109
They can have you know less relevant information they can fix it for themselves.

535
00:37:23,109 --> 00:37:27,191
and then they can ultimately have a lot more in their head at once. I think it's pretty fascinating.

536
00:37:27,200 --> 00:37:35,190
and I think maybe we will have to add some more show notes on time travel debugging.

537
00:37:35,190 --> 00:37:37,829
and examples from some of the blogs that I read on on.

538
00:37:37,829 --> 00:37:40,710
So let me go on to the last portion of the show.

539
00:37:40,710 --> 00:37:44,870
where I want to talk a little bit about autonomous agents for debugging.

540
00:37:44,870 --> 00:37:47,349
and what exactly we mean here.

541
00:37:47,349 --> 00:37:52,630
I want your take because when I think about autonomous agents for debugging.

542
00:37:52,630 --> 00:38:00,230
It appears to me like there's an agent which does the debugging which automatically creates the break points.

543
00:38:00,230 --> 00:38:03,589
which steps through the code finds the issue.

544
00:38:03,589 --> 00:38:08,710
and you know somehow magically displays that on the screen to me.

545
00:38:08,710 --> 00:38:11,190
What is your take on an autonomous agent for debugging?

546
00:38:11,190 --> 00:38:16,710
So, first of all, I'd like to define what I would say an AI agent is.

547
00:38:16,710 --> 00:38:23,349
And it's something that can act on its own independently of you.

548
00:38:23,349 --> 00:38:26,150
So, it can decide to tackle certain tasks.

549
00:38:26,150 --> 00:38:31,670
or run certain tools and then adapt to their responses in pursuit of a wider goal.

550
00:38:31,670 --> 00:38:37,270
And it's doing that autonomously, but on your behalf.

551
00:38:37,270 --> 00:38:39,510
So, it is acting for you in some ways, your agent.

552
00:38:39,510 --> 00:38:46,230
The most common kind of agent we as developers see is the coding agent.

553
00:38:46,230 --> 00:38:48,230
These have sort of evolved from what we called coding assistance.

554
00:38:48,240 --> 00:38:53,190
where it was an incredibly powerful but glorified autocomplete.

555
00:38:53,190 --> 00:38:58,310
into something where it can accomplish software engineering tasks on its own.

556
00:38:58,310 --> 00:39:05,030
That's broadly I think for debugging where things are starting.

557
00:39:05,030 --> 00:39:10,150
Coding agents as we've said debugging is a lot of what coding actually is.

558
00:39:10,150 --> 00:39:12,069
and coding agents have taken that on board as well.

559
00:39:12,069 --> 00:39:17,589
They can do debugging but it's fairly early days.

560
00:39:17,589 --> 00:39:23,750
The interesting thing I see is using a coding agent Claude code for instance.

561
00:39:23,750 --> 00:39:27,030
I've tried to debug a sample problem in the past.

562
00:39:27,030 --> 00:39:28,870
and spoilers I was trying to get it to use a debugger.

563
00:39:28,880 --> 00:39:32,870
because I thought time travel debugging would help.

564
00:39:32,870 --> 00:39:35,910
But early on what I saw it do was edit my code,

565
00:39:35,910 --> 00:39:37,910
add a load of print fs in places it thought was interesting.

566
00:39:37,920 --> 00:39:40,870
and ask for permission to recompile it.

567
00:39:40,880 --> 00:39:43,510
And I mean actually if it can choose the.

568
00:39:43,520 --> 00:39:47,829
right places to put the print fs that's potentially useful.

569
00:39:47,829 --> 00:39:54,630
Again if you have a compilation time that is seconds or minutes rather than hours it's potentially useful.

570
00:39:54,630 --> 00:39:57,910
But it did remind me of a terminator chasing a woolly mammoth.

571
00:39:57,920 --> 00:40:01,750
and trying to bonk it on the head with a bone or something.

572
00:40:01,750 --> 00:40:07,430
is this weird juxtiposition of a very sophisticated modern tool.

573
00:40:07,430 --> 00:40:09,910
and then pretty much the oldest debugging tool we have.

574
00:40:09,920 --> 00:40:19,349
Potentially though, I think we'll see this transition more towards more sophisticated agentic debugging, debugging by agents.

575
00:40:19,349 --> 00:40:24,150
and coding agents are going to be the first place we see that.

576
00:40:24,150 --> 00:40:28,710
thanks to in a large part actually this thing MCP, the model context protocol.

577
00:40:28,710 --> 00:40:34,310
which was developed originally by Anthropic and it's taken off all over the place.

578
00:40:34,310 --> 00:40:36,150
What it amounts to, I would say,

579
00:40:36,150 --> 00:40:40,870
because I spent a lot of time trying to puzzle how it fits into the system.

580
00:40:40,870 --> 00:40:44,630
It's a plug-in architecture kind of no more, no less.

581
00:40:44,630 --> 00:40:47,349
It plugs tools into whatever your local LLM client is.

582
00:40:47,349 --> 00:40:54,470
And there's no reason those tools can't include a debugger or a performance profiler or something else.

583
00:40:54,470 --> 00:40:57,109
The real trick with these tools, though,

584
00:40:57,109 --> 00:41:03,270
is how you get the AI agent to be good at using them.

585
00:41:03,270 --> 00:41:05,910
And that's partly a design challenge for people like me.

586
00:41:05,910 --> 00:41:11,670
So, how do we make our debug tooling work well with what an LLM agent needs?

587
00:41:11,670 --> 00:41:17,510
And it's partly for the AI companies as well to train better tool use into their products.

588
00:41:17,510 --> 00:41:25,349
and more broad awareness of tools, better interaction with the MCP protocol and other tool use protocols.

589
00:41:25,349 --> 00:41:29,109
And what I'd expect we'll see is coding agents getting better and better.

590
00:41:29,109 --> 00:41:36,309
and then potentially specialized agents for debugging certain kinds of problem as well.

591
00:41:36,309 --> 00:41:40,470
because there's a different kind of of knowledge and flow involved in debugging.

592
00:41:40,470 --> 00:41:44,790
You mentioned you know selecting a debugging strategy earlier.

593
00:41:44,790 --> 00:41:46,710
You could imagine a a hierarchical collection of these things.

594
00:41:46,720 --> 00:41:53,991
where maybe your coding agent spits out code and then farms out to a specialized AI. I've got this problem.

595
00:41:53,991 --> 00:41:55,750
how do we solve it?

596
00:41:55,750 --> 00:42:02,230
That tries different strategies, different tools, and aggregates the information together to feed back.

597
00:42:02,230 --> 00:42:05,670
and then the coding agent acts on that, makes some code changes, and we try again.

598
00:42:05,680 --> 00:42:11,349
Yeah, I like that. I think we're still not there based on all of the conversations we've had so far.

599
00:42:11,349 --> 00:42:12,950
But I still had to ask you this.

600
00:42:12,950 --> 00:42:15,109
So do you think the future is something like.

601
00:42:15,109 --> 00:42:19,510
if you have a performance issue which users are reporting.

602
00:42:19,510 --> 00:42:22,550
but you don't really see anything in your traces or logs.

603
00:42:22,550 --> 00:42:29,670
with respect to a performance issue but then it's maybe caused by a third party integration.

604
00:42:29,670 --> 00:42:32,950
Do you think the different debugging agents like you have a master debugging agent.

605
00:42:32,950 --> 00:42:37,349
and you like you said you have lot of mini debugging agents doing different things.

606
00:42:37,349 --> 00:42:43,109
Could this be something like the master orchestrates and finds out the issue in this particular case?

607
00:42:43,109 --> 00:42:47,670
Because this typically happens where the user reports slow performance speeds.

608
00:42:47,670 --> 00:42:52,390
but we have nothing in the logs or any indication in the traces.

609
00:42:52,390 --> 00:42:54,870
to actually show that a particular service is acting badly.

610
00:42:54,870 --> 00:42:58,950
and then you actually find out it's not your service but it's a third party integration.

611
00:42:58,960 --> 00:43:05,990
I think so this is probably not possible yet but we'll already be seeing the glimmers of it.

612
00:43:05,990 --> 00:43:11,430
And actually I think one thing which is worth bearing in mind is that.

613
00:43:11,430 --> 00:43:15,910
there's a perfect rosecolored spectacles world where the AIs solve all of our problems.

614
00:43:15,910 --> 00:43:17,829
and they can solve these things end to end.

615
00:43:17,829 --> 00:43:23,191
But actually there's huge value to be had in getting them to do the boring 80% of the. work.

616
00:43:23,191 --> 00:43:25,829
to take the toil off so we can focus.

617
00:43:25,829 --> 00:43:30,470
So even if we can't solve the whole thing, actually having the LLM act as your agent again.

618
00:43:30,480 --> 00:43:37,750
go out and gather the information you need to make the next decision still hugely valuable.

619
00:43:37,750 --> 00:43:45,911
I think the trick to debugging issues is how you make the AI as smart as it can. be.

620
00:43:45,911 --> 00:43:48,710
And the challenge for an AI debugging agent is.

621
00:43:48,710 --> 00:43:51,910
that you have to get the right context fed in there.

622
00:43:51,910 --> 00:43:55,510
just like a human developer, but more.

623
00:43:55,510 --> 00:43:57,750
so they need to know as much as possible about what's going on.

624
00:43:57,750 --> 00:44:04,069
because they otherwise they won't be able to answer questions or direct their investigations.

625
00:44:04,069 --> 00:44:06,790
And if they don't know stuff, they also tend to hallucinate.

626
00:44:06,800 --> 00:44:10,069
And that's something I think we've all seen at this point.

627
00:44:10,069 --> 00:44:13,430
Sometimes it's very amusing, but you don't want it happening in the middle of a production issue.

628
00:44:13,430 --> 00:44:15,829
and sending you on a wild goose trace.

629
00:44:15,829 --> 00:44:20,150
So for this, you need the right ability to gather the information.

630
00:44:20,150 --> 00:44:22,550
and you need that information to be solid.

631
00:44:22,550 --> 00:44:29,829
So in the kind of scenario you described, I'd imagine this is this is probably a tiered approach.

632
00:44:29,829 --> 00:44:31,910
Often debugging challenges want a range of tools.

633
00:44:31,910 --> 00:44:39,990
So you might start with your inputs are basic performance level monitoring and user input.

634
00:44:39,990 --> 00:44:41,589
So user feedback is valuable as well here.

635
00:44:41,589 --> 00:44:50,950
Once you've started investigating, I'd imagine you'd go down potentially a chain of increasingly sophisticated debug approaches.

636
00:44:50,950 --> 00:44:57,271
So you'd initially look at your tracing and you might well automate that and say, "Okay, LLM, when a performance alert goes off.

637
00:44:57,280 --> 00:45:03,109
look at the traces, see if there's anything weird." If there's not, then you've got a choice, I guess.

638
00:45:03,109 --> 00:45:05,670
You can go and look as a human,

639
00:45:05,670 --> 00:45:07,589
or you can say, "Okay, go and do the next phase."

640
00:45:07,589 --> 00:45:11,990
And the next phase in that world would probably be something like profiling.

641
00:45:11,990 --> 00:45:17,510
or some lightweight capture of more detail tracing.

642
00:45:17,510 --> 00:45:19,750
But if you've got a complex problem with many moving parts.

643
00:45:19,750 --> 00:45:23,589
or maybe you've got legacy parts of the system, maybe that's not enough as well.

644
00:45:23,589 --> 00:45:28,550
So at that point, you might move up to two potential approaches.

645
00:45:28,550 --> 00:45:32,391
One is write tests and try and replicate it out of production. That may or may not work.

646
00:45:32,391 --> 00:45:36,791
or for bugs where it's extremely complicated. You can't replicate it.

647
00:45:36,791 --> 00:45:38,630
It only happens in this place.

648
00:45:38,630 --> 00:45:42,790
That's that's where I'd say something like a time travel debugging system.

649
00:45:42,790 --> 00:45:49,190
with its capability to fully record and capture the interactions between different services as.

650
00:45:49,190 --> 00:45:51,510
well would be really valuable.

651
00:45:51,510 --> 00:45:53,750
So I think the LLM can help with the individual stages.

652
00:45:53,750 --> 00:46:02,390
but ultimately the challenge we are facing at each stage is how do we make the LLM for.

653
00:46:02,390 --> 00:46:04,230
this part of the task as smart as possible.

654
00:46:04,230 --> 00:46:06,870
So that's down to prompting giving it the right details.

655
00:46:06,870 --> 00:46:13,030
and giving it then a ground truth about what it's reasoning about and giving it the right context.

656
00:46:13,030 --> 00:46:14,550
And the ultimate of that is when you get up to the full logs.

657
00:46:14,550 --> 00:46:16,390
that come out of time travel debugging.

658
00:46:16,390 --> 00:46:20,870
and it gives you the ability to verify what went through the system as well.

659
00:46:20,870 --> 00:46:23,109
and why things happened.

660
00:46:23,109 --> 00:46:25,190
So the LLM's got the power of.

661
00:46:25,190 --> 00:46:27,990
that but you've got the power as well to go through and check it's working.

662
00:46:28,000 --> 00:46:29,829
I think that makes a lot of sense.

663
00:46:29,829 --> 00:46:34,309
The answer that you said it has to be a layered approach.

664
00:46:34,309 --> 00:46:38,390
So let's move on to the next question I wanted to ask you is one of the worries.

665
00:46:38,390 --> 00:46:40,309
you have with autonomous agents.

666
00:46:40,309 --> 00:46:45,349
is you know introducing any regressions or security vulnerabilities.

667
00:46:45,349 --> 00:46:47,670
or maybe masking the real root cause.

668
00:46:47,670 --> 00:46:52,870
The reason I asked this question is recently I remember seeing this thread on Twitter.

669
00:46:52,870 --> 00:46:55,030
which I think orex.

670
00:46:55,030 --> 00:46:57,589
which I'm sure a lot of you also saw.

671
00:46:57,589 --> 00:46:59,750
which is with one of the databases from one of the companies.

672
00:46:59,750 --> 00:47:03,670
where a lot of records were deleted.

673
00:47:03,670 --> 00:47:07,990
and then when the autonomous agent was posed because that agent actually produced the different.

674
00:47:07,990 --> 00:47:11,750
it inserted rows into the table and then deleted a lot of it.

675
00:47:11,750 --> 00:47:17,190
and when you ask the agent probe the agent about this deletion it actually lied about it.

676
00:47:17,190 --> 00:47:21,829
and actually came up with some fake records which also happened.

677
00:47:21,829 --> 00:47:23,829
So this is a cause of concern.

