00:47:23,829 --> 00:47:25,750
Obviously one of the things the team did in that case.

679
00:47:25,750 --> 00:47:30,230
I think they've added a lot of guard rails around what the agent could do.

680
00:47:30,230 --> 00:47:32,069
and how much access it had and things like that.

681
00:47:32,079 --> 00:47:38,150
Now when you're actually sort of looking at those autonomous switch agents in the debugging context.

682
00:47:38,150 --> 00:47:42,870
and where we're trying to solve a problem again we could run into similar issues right.

683
00:47:42,870 --> 00:47:46,630
where you don't really how much do you believe the agent there's a certain level of trust.

684
00:47:46,640 --> 00:47:50,230
but I wanted to kind of I had to pose this question to you to ask you.

685
00:47:50,230 --> 00:47:54,309
what do you need to do to validate that what the agent is giving is actually right.

686
00:47:54,319 --> 00:47:57,750
Sure.

687
00:47:57,750 --> 00:48:02,230
It's an interesting one because yes, there's so many nightmare scenarios out there.

688
00:48:02,230 --> 00:48:07,911
where you see somebody who said conversations like, "Why is the database empty? Why did you delete it?"

689
00:48:07,920 --> 00:48:09,910
And the LLM says, "You're right.

690
00:48:09,910 --> 00:48:14,950
You did tell me specifically not to delete the entire database.

691
00:48:14,950 --> 00:48:16,870
Next time, I will ensure that that doesn't happen."

692
00:48:16,880 --> 00:48:22,790
There's lots of opportunity for unexpected behavior still.

693
00:48:22,790 --> 00:48:29,430
Ultimately, the AI model vendors, I think, do a lot of work to try and mitigate this stuff.

694
00:48:29,440 --> 00:48:33,750
They do a lot of work with reinforcement learning to try and align the AI.

695
00:48:33,750 --> 00:48:39,589
with don't lie to the user and don't do inadvisable things.

696
00:48:39,589 --> 00:48:42,549
Let's say follow instructions carefully.

697
00:48:42,549 --> 00:48:47,670
But the problem with them is right now it's not exactly that they're lying.

698
00:48:47,670 --> 00:48:49,589
Even they don't know they're lying.

699
00:48:49,589 --> 00:48:51,589
They know the thing they do.

700
00:48:51,589 --> 00:48:54,150
And the thing they do is try and provide you a good answer.

701
00:48:54,150 --> 00:48:56,069
And there are many parts of a good answer.

702
00:48:56,069 --> 00:49:00,549
One of them is having an authorative and polite tone.

703
00:49:00,549 --> 00:49:02,630
Another is using the correct terminology for your domain.

704
00:49:02,630 --> 00:49:06,069
Another is citing specific examples from your source code.

705
00:49:06,069 --> 00:49:08,549
And another is actually being based in truth.

706
00:49:08,549 --> 00:49:12,790
And they'll choose as many as those as they can to get you to a good answer.

707
00:49:12,790 --> 00:49:14,870
But any of them might get dropped.

708
00:49:14,870 --> 00:49:18,150
And one of the hard ones to keep is the truth.

709
00:49:18,150 --> 00:49:23,109
So that that is quite likely to be a casualty if there's not enough information.

710
00:49:23,109 --> 00:49:25,990
As we said earlier, I think guard rails are very important.

711
00:49:26,000 --> 00:49:29,349
And there's two ways you can interpret the rails as well.

712
00:49:29,349 --> 00:49:34,790
There's the there's the rails which stop you, you know, tripping over somewhere you shouldn't.

713
00:49:34,800 --> 00:49:36,870
The safety rails.

714
00:49:36,870 --> 00:49:42,230
So that would be things like controls on what operations the AI can do.

715
00:49:42,230 --> 00:49:46,470
The other is more like train tracks not in the sense of exactly controlling it.

716
00:49:46,470 --> 00:49:52,549
but in the sense of choosing desirable paths so providing the right information to them.

717
00:49:52,549 --> 00:49:59,430
So I guess if we look at the context of introducing security vulnerabilities.

718
00:49:59,440 --> 00:50:01,670
let's say you might have a guardrail.

719
00:50:01,680 --> 00:50:08,630
which is certain kinds of security scanner that run automatically as in in static checks.

720
00:50:08,630 --> 00:50:13,349
So providing that feedback path, that feedback path to an agent is very important.

721
00:50:13,349 --> 00:50:18,150
because it's how it learns about the world you've put it in.

722
00:50:18,150 --> 00:50:22,710
In terms of regressions, I'm afraid the answer there is going to be testing as it always is.

723
00:50:22,710 --> 00:50:30,069
Better development practices help as well though, and that includes better development practices for the AI.

724
00:50:30,069 --> 00:50:31,990
So any static checks you can do will help.

725
00:50:31,990 --> 00:50:34,069
Turning on all of your compiler warnings will help.

726
00:50:34,069 --> 00:50:38,630
and also anything you can do to help it understand the real context.

727
00:50:38,630 --> 00:50:44,069
So there's another interesting we talked about DBG.

728
00:50:44,069 --> 00:50:47,750
There's another interesting project called LDB which I think is LLM debugger.

729
00:50:47,760 --> 00:50:56,391
and that's written about in a paper from the University of California San Diego called debug like a. human.

730
00:50:56,391 --> 00:51:04,390
and the subtitle there is a large language model debugger via verifying runtime execution step by step.

731
00:51:04,390 --> 00:51:05,910
and they showed something actually really interesting.

732
00:51:05,910 --> 00:51:11,109
which was that they gave an LLM that was being used as a coding agent.

733
00:51:11,109 --> 00:51:13,430
the ability to step through code it had just generated.

734
00:51:13,440 --> 00:51:17,589
and look and see if it did what it expected.

735
00:51:17,589 --> 00:51:19,910
or if it had violated invariance that it expected to be there.

736
00:51:19,920 --> 00:51:27,510
And what they've shown is that actually giving a coding agent better insight into how the code they.

737
00:51:27,510 --> 00:51:30,631
wrote behaves dynamically. can make them smarter.

738
00:51:30,631 --> 00:51:35,190
So, I think there's a whole world here again in providing better kinds of context.

739
00:51:35,190 --> 00:51:39,990
and better kinds of ground truth into an AI system.

740
00:51:39,990 --> 00:51:44,549
because ultimately if you get that right, the AI become even smarter than they already are.

741
00:51:44,549 --> 00:51:46,790
They're already very good at coding.

742
00:51:46,790 --> 00:51:48,390
But if if you can point them in the right direction.

743
00:51:48,390 --> 00:51:51,990
and give them the things they really need to know.

744
00:51:51,990 --> 00:51:53,670
actually you can unlock more of that capability.

745
00:51:53,670 --> 00:51:57,750
and you can be using their intelligence for the right things which is writing your code.

746
00:51:57,750 --> 00:52:01,750
instead of the wrong things which is puzzling through gaps in the data.

747
00:52:01,750 --> 00:52:04,069
that you could easily get for them.

748
00:52:04,069 --> 00:52:06,470
The last thing masking the real root cause.

749
00:52:06,470 --> 00:52:11,030
and actually I think this applies for regressions and security vulnerabilities as well.

750
00:52:11,030 --> 00:52:15,990
is I'm afraid people don't usually like it but code review you still got to do it.

751
00:52:16,000 --> 00:52:20,069
You've still got somebody maybe with AI assistants as well.

752
00:52:20,069 --> 00:52:21,910
but ultimately somebody's still got to check.

753
00:52:21,910 --> 00:52:26,870
that the tests don't pass now simply because the LLM deleted all of them.

754
00:52:26,870 --> 00:52:31,270
or the LLM didn't put in an obvious backdoor into the system.

755
00:52:31,270 --> 00:52:35,910
in the interest of making something else it thought you wanted possible.

756
00:52:35,910 --> 00:52:37,910
So I think there's got to be for the foreseeable future something.

757
00:52:37,920 --> 00:52:41,910
that looks like our modern software development life cycle.

758
00:52:41,910 --> 00:52:44,390
maybe AI assisted but humans in the loop.

759
00:52:44,390 --> 00:52:48,710
Humans ultimately responsible for making sure this stuff is right.

760
00:52:48,710 --> 00:52:53,109
and that the right code is written to match the the end users requirements.

761
00:52:53,119 --> 00:52:57,270
I think that's great. I think that's a very valid point.

762
00:52:57,270 --> 00:52:59,829
How do you trust an output and verify.

763
00:52:59,829 --> 00:53:04,711
have some sort of a human in the loop to check the validity of the output. also where possible?

764
00:53:04,711 --> 00:53:06,390
Yeah, I think that's great.

765
00:53:06,390 --> 00:53:08,549
I think that brings us to the end of our show.

766
00:53:08,549 --> 00:53:15,829
So it's been a fascinating conversation where we went right from treating the debugger as an assistant tooling.

767
00:53:15,829 --> 00:53:18,309
to also looking at it being autonomous.

768
